import { BytesReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesReaderCopy = BytesReaderCopy {
  summary: "base64.encodeUrl turns Bytes into URL-safe Base64. It uses - and _ instead of standard + and /, and adds no trailing = padding.",
  taskTitle: "Write URL-safe Base64 without padding",
  purpose: "Put the two byte values 251 and 255 into a field requiring URL-safe Base64. Base64 represents bytes with a limited set of characters. Here you produce -_8 and check that decodeUrl can read it back using the same format.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript puts the two fixed bytes in a Uint8Array and writes them with toBase64({ alphabet: \"base64url\", omitPadding: true }). alphabet selects the character set containing - and _, while omitPadding leaves off trailing =. The decoder re-encodes in the same format and compares with its input to reject alternate spellings a permissive decoder could read.",
  benefit: "TypeScript’s standard API can produce the same representation. Seseragi puts the URL-safe alphabet and no-padding choice together in the name encodeUrl, converting Bytes to String. The matching decodeUrl returns success and failure as distinct values.",
  setup: "bytes, base64, and hex alias std/bytes, std/bytes/base64, and std/bytes/hex respectively. Bytes is an immutable sequence of values from 0 through 255. hex is used to inspect successfully decoded byte values as hexadecimal.",
  after: [
    "fn encoded -> String defines a no-argument function returning a string; encoded () calls it. fn introduces the definition and -> String gives its result type. bytes.fromInts [251, 255] checks the integer array and returns Left error on failure or Right content on success. Either is the type for these two possible results.",
    "match chooses the branch for the result received. Left error uses show error to turn the reason into a string; Right content passes the successful Bytes to base64.encodeUrl content. Calls separate the function name and argument with a space.",
    "fn read input: String -> String turns a string named input into a display string. : String gives the argument type. For decodeUrl, Left _ ignores the reason and returns Rejected, while Right content uses hex.encode content to show the bytes. + joins strings.",
    "The initial -_8 represents [251, 255], and fbff on the decoded line represents the same two bytes. The empty string is also accepted. AA=, +A, AB, and A are rejected for padding, a character from the other alphabet, unused trailing bits, and length, respectively. The for array contains strings returned by calls such as encoded () and read \"-_8\"; it prints them in order.",
  ],
  details: [
    "encodeUrl takes one Bytes value and returns a new String. It uses A–Z, a–z, 0–9, -, and _, with no =, whitespace, or line breaks. Empty Bytes becomes an empty string.",
    "Both the alphabet and padding differ from standard encode. Use encode if the receiver requires standard Base64. URL-safe does not mean that this operation builds an entire URL or URL-escapes an arbitrary string.",
    "encodeUrl itself does not return a failure result. The example’s Left/Right branches handle fromInts, which constructs Bytes from numbers, and decodeUrl, which reads bytes from text. To send text, first create Bytes with encodeUtf8 from std/text.",
    "URL-safe Base64 is neither encryption nor a signature. Giving a token this representation does not keep its contents secret or verify its authenticity. Conversion creates a value from the entire input.",
  ],
  nodeNote: "The inspected Node 24 runtime has no toBase64()/fromBase64(). With Node/Bun, import { Buffer } from \"node:buffer\" and write Buffer.from(content).toString(\"base64url\"). Buffer.from(input, \"base64url\") is permissive, so also require an exact match between the input and the result re-encoded as base64url.",
}
